{"type":"message","id":"f94aa720","parentId":"42a1d6a6","timestamp":"2026-10-17T22:45:56.984Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `parse_date` in `dates/parse.py`, and all 3 tests in `tests/test_parse.py` now pass, including `test_dates`.\n\n**Cause:** the old pattern `\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S\"` has no field for a timezone, so the trailing `Z` in `\"2026-10-12T08:30:00Z\"` was left over and raised the error.\n\n**Fix:** `parse_date` now uses `datetime.fromisoformat(s)` instead of the fixed pattern. On Python 3.11+ (this machine has 3.13.5), that handles `Z` and other offsets like `+02:00`, as well as timestamps with no timezone.\n\n**Behaviour changes to know about:**\n- **Timezone-aware results:** timestamps ending in `Z` or an offset now come back with a timezone (`Z` gives UTC); timestamps without one still come back without one. Python raises `TypeError` if you compare or subtract the two kinds, so any code that mixes them will fail. If you'd rather always get timezone-free UTC times, the fix is to convert to UTC and drop the timezone after parsing.\n- **Looser input:** it also accepts other ISO-8601 forms, such as a date with no time or fractional seconds, which the old pattern rejected.\n- **Older Python:** on 3.10 or earlier it still rejects `Z`."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":459,"cacheRead":26567,"cacheWrite":130,"totalTokens":27158,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":130}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792277151669,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011Cg8cyonrD4g6pUdMcQBxw","duration":5314.072713000001,"ttft":5288.456342000001,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":26699,"nonMessageTokens":21589,"compactionEpoch":0}}}
